status: done (2026-07-10). T1..T6 green at all layers.

T1 (scala unit) confirmed a query string with embedded double quotes and
underscores ("\"_ + _ = _\"") crosses tools/call -> backend.ir intact,
quote characters included; max_results plumbs through the same way as
sledgehammer's timeout_secs (json_args already omits the pair when the
client omits the argument, no special handler code needed).

T2 (ml unit) confirmed a malformed query (an unterminated quote) is a
clean status error from Find_Theorems.read_query, not a crash.

T3 (ml unit, pinned -- a genuine surprise): Find_Theorems.find_theorems_cmd
returns opt_found = NONE whenever a limit cuts the search short (the
exhaustive total is unknown at that point), so a BOUNDED query's tally
is "displaying N theorem(s)", never the optimistic "found N (M
displayed)" shape one might assume from ir.ML's own tally-formatting
code reading in isolation -- the found/displayed split only fires when
the search is unlimited (max_results 0) and completes exhaustively,
giving "found N theorem(s)" with N lines actually returned. Both shapes
are pinned in MCP_Repl_Tests.thy with "name:conj" (Main has more than
3 matches, so max_results=3 truncates and max_results=0 does not).

T4 (ml unit) confirmed goal-based criteria ("intro") error cleanly at
theory level (no goal to search from) and, once mid-proof on a
conjunction goal, return plausible rules including conjI.

T5 (e2e) confirmed the description's quoting contract is livable
without mcp_server.py's python-side auto-quoter: name:conjI (bare,
unquoted) and "_ + _ = _ + _" (quoted term pattern) both return hits
through the full stack -- the auto-quoting heuristic was deliberately
NOT reimplemented scala-side (recorded in the CHANGELOG).

T6 (bridge) confirmed find_theorems on one REPL returns promptly while
a slow 2s step is still in flight on a different REPL -- it needs no
async gymnastics of its own (unlike sledgehammer), but must not (and
does not) queue behind an unrelated slow call.

ml unit: MCP_Repl_Tests.thy; scala unit: isabelle mcp_test; bridge
(T6): isabelle mcp_test -b -d mcp/Tools; e2e (T5): test_mcp.py.

context promotion (decided 2026-07-12): repl? | theory?
--------------------------------------------------------

status: implemented (2026-07-12) — the shipped tool above required repl;
this section is the delta. spec: "proof search", promotion note.

implementation notes: the default context (neither repl nor theory) is
MCP_Repl's OWN captured self-theory (MCP_Repl.set_self_theory, mirroring
ir.ML's internal Ir.set_self_theory/the_self_theory, which is not
exported by ir.ML's signature and so unusable from here directly — a
second, independent capture of the same \<^theory> value, same file-level
positioning). Theory-context and default-context search share one new
helper, find_theorems_ctxt (MCP_Repl.thy), which wraps
Find_Theorems.find_theorems_cmd's own "Current goal required" error with
"-- no goal: use a repl mid-proof" (both branches, T10). scala-side,
resolve_context_theory (MCP_Backend trait) resolves the client's theory
spelling to the canonical Thy_Info key via resolve_theory (image/loaded
tiers only; filesystem-tier and unknown both error before crossing the
bridge). T7..T10 green in MCP_Repl_Tests.thy plus mcp_handler_tests.scala
plus a live e2e check (find_theorems with zero repls on a fresh server).

motivation: the search needs a context, not proof state — requiring
repl_init before any semantic search was the one repl-bound hole in
the otherwise repl-free read-only surface. the fix mirrors
find_definition's already-specced context selectors exactly, so the
two finding tools present the same interface.

metadata delta (the builtin table row)
--------------------------------------

inputSchema   required drops to ["query"]; add
                "theory": {"type": "string"}
              repl and theory are mutually exclusive — handler-
              enforced, same as find_definition and
              repl_init_from_source's locators.
description   append the context contract: "Context: pass `repl` to
              search that REPL's current context (goal-aware
              mid-proof — needed for intro/elim/dest/solves), or
              `theory` for a loaded/image theory's global context;
              default is the base image. Goal-based criteria require
              a REPL that is mid-proof."
annotations   unchanged (readOnly, idempotent, closed-world).

changes required, by layer
--------------------------

1. scala (mcp_server.scala builtin row + handler): schema/description
   as above; exclusivity check (repl + theory both given -> isError,
   before dispatch); when theory is given, normalize the client
   spelling through image_theory (the resolver from the entities fix,
   mcp_session.scala) and forward the RESOLVED canonical key —
   theory-name spelling rule, same as every other theory-taking
   surface. omit absent optional pairs (json_args does this already).
2. ml (MCP_Repl.thy dispatcher — ir.ML stays verbatim, Amazon-header
   constraint): the "find_theorems" entry grows context resolution
   instead of passing straight to Ir.find_theorems:
     repl given    -> Ir.find_theorems as today (last state's
                      context, goal-aware).
     theory given  -> Thy_Info.get_theory (canonical key) |>
                      Proof_Context.init_global; run
                      Find_Theorems.read_query + find_theorems_cmd
                      against it with no goal — new ML alongside the
                      entities function, reusing ir.ML's tally
                      formatting shape (out "...").
     neither       -> the startup theory's global context (same
                      default find_definition specs).
   goal-based criteria at theory level keep erroring cleanly (T4
   pinned that); the error text should now say "no goal — use a repl
   mid-proof".
3. keys: dispatcher key set becomes ["repl", "theory", "query",
   "max_results"]; missing-repl is no longer an error path.

